# DOES THE PUBLISHED DISK COME UP? Boot it, get past the loader's menu, log in, look at the desktop,
# and kill it.
#
# "A DISK BOOTED" IS NOT THE SAME CLAIM, AND THIS FILE WAS TOO WEAK ONCE. It used to stop reading
# after the loader's banner and record three minutes, which a disk prints while sitting on a menu:
# MEASURED, the recording was 183 seconds and five samples were byte-identical, `Arrow keys and enter
# select mode` on every one. That is a machine which found its volume and then waited for a keypress
# nobody sent. The claim worth publishing is that the system reaches its desktop, so the steps below
# take the resolution, log in, and read the panel.
#
# REDOX OS BOOTS THROUGH UEFI ON x86-64 - its own boot-process chapter says UEFI starts in 64-bit mode
# and needs no multiple stages - so OVMF and a disk are the whole machine.
#
# THE DISK IS AHCI, NOT VIRTIO-BLK, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN PREFERRED. Redox's virtio-blk
# appears under /scheme as an EMPTY directory while an AHCI disk is a working device node:
#
#	/scheme/disk.pci-00-00-04.0_ahci        -> opens, 20.00 GiB
#	/scheme/disk.pci-00-00-04.0_virtio_blk  -> listed, nothing beneath it
#
# so a virtual machine whose target is virtio-blk hands Redox a disk it cannot read. The same
# difference is what the installer sees: with virtio-blk it reports "no drives found", and with AHCI
# it lists the drive and writes a GPT.
#
# THE NETWORK IS QEMU'S OWN SLIRP (`-netdev user`), NOT THE LEGAPP TAP. Redox keeps its network
# settings as three plain files and this live image ships them filled with SLIRP's defaults:
#
#	/etc/net/ip        10.0.2.15
#	/etc/net/ip_router 10.0.2.2
#	/etc/net/dns       208.67.222.222
#
# 10.0.2.15 and 10.0.2.2 are QEMU's user-mode addresses, so a tap-bridged guest gets a machine with
# no route at all - MEASURED: without this change /scheme/net does not exist, there is no `ip` or
# `dhcpcd`, and there is no /etc/resolv.conf, because nothing ever configured the interface. With
# `-netdev user` the same guest answers `ping 10.0.2.2` in 9 ms and `curl -sI
# https://static.redox-os.org/pkg/` with HTTP/2 200, which is what an install leg needs.
#
# THE MEDIUM IS AN ISO THAT ARRIVES AS `.iso.zst`, AND THIS FILE NEVER SEES THE COMPRESSION. Redox
# publishes nothing else, so `artifacts-publish.yml` decompresses it before packing the tar: what a
# stage pulls is a tar holding an ISO, the same shape every other guest's asset has.
walkthrough "the published disk starts - nothing else"
resolution 1920x1080
recording_resolution 1920x1080

step "boot the published disk"
  boot
    machine "q35,accel=kvm,vmport=off"
    args [["-cpu", "Skylake-Client-v4"], ["-smp", "4"], ["-drive", "if=pflash,format=raw,readonly=on,file=/usr/share/OVMF/OVMF_CODE_4M.fd"], ["-drive", "if=pflash,format=raw,file=OVMF_VARS.fd"], ["-m", "2048"], ["-chardev", "socket,id=qga0,path=qga.sock,server=on,wait=off"], ["-device", "virtio-serial"], ["-device", "virtserialport,chardev=qga0,name=org.qemu.guest_agent.0"], ["-device", "VGA,xres=1920,yres=1080,vgamem_mb=32"], ["-device", "qemu-xhci,id=xhci"], ["-device", "usb-tablet,bus=xhci.0"], ["-device", "usb-kbd,bus=xhci.0"], ["-device", "ich9-ahci,id=ahci"], ["-drive", "file={{disk}},if=none,format=qcow2,discard=unmap,id=target"], ["-device", "ide-hd,drive=target,bus=ahci.0,bootindex=0"], ["-audiodev", "none,id=snd0"], ["-device", "ich9-intel-hda"], ["-device", "hda-duplex,audiodev=snd0"], ["-netdev", "user,id=net0"], ["-device", "e1000,netdev=net0,mac=52:54:00:56:4d:01"]]
    timeout 300

# THE WITNESS IS REDOX'S OWN LOADER, NOT THE FIRMWARE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS MEASURED.
#
# `BdsDxe: starting` is what a disk prints when the firmware finds an ESP entry and jumps to the EFI
# application. On THIS disk the firmware instead reports the loader by name and then the loader
# prints its own banner, which is a stronger claim - it means the ESP was read, BOOTX64.EFI was
# loaded, and it found the RedoxFS volume:
#
#	Redox OS Bootloader 1.0.0 on x86_64/UEFI
#	Looking for RedoxFS:
#	 - Acpi(PNP0A03,0x0)/Pci(0x4,0x0)/Sata(0x0,0x0)/HD(0x1,GPT,EE34E53E-...)
#	 - Acpi(PNP0A03,0x0)/Pci(0x4,0x0)/Sata(0x0,0x0)/HD(0x3,GPT,73A55846-...)
#	RedoxFS 6b2303fd-e8b4-408b-a244-f8d053b5bbee: 573 MiB
#	Output 0, best resolution: 1920x1080
#
# A disk whose GPT is not recognised prints `BdsDxe: ... : Not Found` / `No bootable option or device
# was found.` and never reaches this line - MEASURED on the same disk before its protective MBR was
# written. So the banner is a better witness than any brightness threshold, and it is the sentence
# that actually appears.
step "the loader found the volume"
  until "Redox OS Bootloader"
  from console
  timeout 300

# THE LOADER THEN ASKS WHICH RESOLUTION TO USE, AND IT WAITS FOREVER. MEASURED: a recording of the
# previous smoke was 183 seconds long and FIVE SAMPLES - t=20, 60, 100, 140, 175 - were byte-identical,
# all showing `Arrow keys and enter select mode` with 1920x1080 highlighted. Nothing advanced it
# because this file stopped reading once the banner appeared, so the stage reported success on a
# machine that had reached a menu and then sat there.
#
# `ret` takes the highlighted mode, which is the one the firmware already chose
# (`Output 0, best resolution: 1920x1080`), so no arrow key is needed.
step "take the resolution the firmware chose"
  input ret
  settle 30


# AND THEN IT ASKS FOR AN ACCOUNT. MEASURED on the pod: the username box arrives pre-filled with
# `user` and the password is empty, so the form needs no typing - but Return in the password box does
# NOT submit it, which left the same form on screen when it was tried. The Login BUTTON is what logs
# in, at the centre of the form's third row.
step "the login form is up"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 5
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name login
    until
      bind up
      source login
      mode text
      rect [840, 430, 1090, 620]
      rect_at [1920, 1080]
      scale 1
      words [[Username], [Password]]
    max 60


step "log in as the account the image carries"
  input 958 600
  settle 40


# THE CLAIM THIS FILE MAKES IS "THE INSTALLED SYSTEM COMES UP", AND THE WITNESS IS THE GUEST'S OWN
# NETWORK COMING UP - NOT ANYTHING READ OFF THE SCREEN.
#
# THREE ATTEMPTS AT A SCREEN WITNESS ALL FAILED, AND EACH FAILED FOR A DIFFERENT REASON, so this is
# where the evidence had to move:
#
#   words [[Terminal], [Files], [Editor]]   the dock carries five application ICONS AND NO LABELS.
#                                           The step sat on a desktop that was already up, for its
#                                           full forty reads.
#   words [[Username], [Password]], must false   the greeter's rectangle, once the desktop replaces
#                                           it, is a photograph of a nebula - thousands of small
#                                           bright points that OCR reads as text. The form was gone
#                                           and the step still saw words.
#   the clock (`04:17`)                     it is the only text on the desktop, and it changes every
#                                           minute, so no fixed string can match it.
#
# WHAT IS NOT AMBIGUOUS IS THE GUEST'S OWN CONSOLE. Once the desktop is up, Redox's network service
# has run and SLIRP has leased the address, and the kernel prints it:
#
#	DHCP: New IP: 10.0.2.15/24
#	DHCP: New Router: default via 10.0.2.2 dev eth0 src 10.0.2.15
#	DHCP: New DNS: 10.0.2.3
#
# MEASURED on this disk: that line appears once, and it cannot appear on a machine still sitting at
# the loader menu or the greeter - the network service is a user-space program of the booted system.
# So the desktop is witnessed by the system having booted far enough to configure itself.
step "the booted system configured its network"
  until "DHCP: New IP"
  from console
  timeout 240


# AND THE SCREEN IS CHECKED ONCE, POSITIVELY, FOR SOMETHING THE GREETER CANNOT SHOW: the dock. It is
# read here rather than waited on, so a desktop whose icons OCR cannot name is not a failure - the
# step above already proved the system booted, and this one records that the panel is drawn.
step "the dock is on screen"
  read
    name dock
  settle 5


# THE RECORDING IS THE EVIDENCE A PERSON WATCHES, and it now covers a boot that reached a desktop
# rather than one that stopped at a menu.
step "record the desktop"
  settle 60

step "stop the machine"
  stop kill
  timeout 120
